WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.050
- We have our first question from Juan.

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:05.450
Thank you for sending in another question.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:07.348
We, I remember hearing from you last week.

00:00:07.348 --> 00:00:09.959
So, Juan wants to know if the study is

00:00:09.959 --> 00:00:12.720
longitudinal, since...

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:21.514
- [Odaelys] I think
Jeanne went out for a bit.

00:00:21.514 --> 00:00:22.410
- [Pedro] Oops did we lose Jeanne?

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:23.243
- Yeah.

00:00:23.243 --> 00:00:24.140
(laughs)

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.141
- That's all right, I can take over.

00:00:26.141 --> 00:00:28.760
Well, let me start by once
again thanking you Odaelys,

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.719
that was a really really
fantastic presentation.

00:00:31.719 --> 00:00:34.930
And, I'll pick up from
where Jeanne left off,

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:36.480
on the question from Juan,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.720
who is asking if the
study was longitudinal

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.300
because the sample size has changed

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.823
between each range of phase
that you were showing.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:46.945
- So the--
- [Jeanne] I'm back.

00:00:46.945 --> 00:00:47.970
- Oh!

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:48.803
- [Jeanne] Yeah, sorry.

00:00:48.803 --> 00:00:50.060
(group laughs)

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:53.173
We all face internet problems, right?

00:00:53.173 --> 00:00:55.410
I think that this question actually is

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.880
like why are there different
numbers of patients.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.218
Like its not the same--

00:01:00.218 --> 00:01:01.051
- It's not.

00:01:01.051 --> 00:01:02.050
And because they are looking at,

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.570
they're asking a different questions.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.230
So in that first figure that I showed you

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.160
where they're just
looking at the presence,

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:08.430
or the detectable amounts of antibodies.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:12.520
They are looking just to see
if all of the 285 patients,

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:17.070
if they're able to see if they
have IgM or IgG antibodies.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.490
Now, out of those patients,

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:22.070
you can't really look
at when the time course

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:23.840
of the production of the antibodies.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.340
So they then took out data sets

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.420
where they have multiple serum samples.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.340
So about 200 of the patients,

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.700
they only had one blood
sample from the patient

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.243
that they are able to analyze from,

00:01:34.243 --> 00:01:36.687
and from that one sample you can only see,

00:01:36.687 --> 00:01:39.550
"Oh I see IgM, oh I see IgG",

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:42.188
and they use that in a particular graph.

00:01:42.188 --> 00:01:43.740
In the other data sets

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.080
where they are looking
at it from being negative

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.655
for certain antibodies to being positive,

00:01:48.655 --> 00:01:50.020
(birds chirping)
they can only use the ones

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.000
that they have multiple serum samples.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:52.841
So yes,

00:01:52.841 --> 00:01:55.830
the samples differ based on the questions

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:57.280
that they were trying to ask.

00:02:00.514 --> 00:02:01.347
I think that--

00:02:01.347 --> 00:02:02.370
- [Jeanne] Cool, thanks, and Odaelys,

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:05.320
I can hear your bluejays in your backyard.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.510
- Ooh yeah, I know I've been like--

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.930
I've been using the phone
to detect what birds are--

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.226
- [Jeanne] So loud (laughs), yeah

00:02:12.226 --> 00:02:13.710
- [Pedro] Isn't it lovely though?

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:14.933
- Yes it is actually.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.620
- Okay well let's go
for question two then,

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.483
from Urania Davis who asks

00:02:22.483 --> 00:02:25.128
how long the IgG needs to be present

00:02:25.128 --> 00:02:28.330
in order for us to
consider a patient immune.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:29.859
- We have no idea.

00:02:29.859 --> 00:02:30.692
(laughs)

00:02:30.692 --> 00:02:33.267
And so yeah we're really trying,

00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:35.270
well not me in particular,

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:39.400
but scientists are really
trying hard to figure out

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:43.140
if one, does it-- is there difference with

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.160
how much IgG antibodies you're producing?

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.410
Does it matter if the IgG antibodies

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:50.350
are neutralizing or not?

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:54.950
And also do the antibodies
do seem to decrease overtime?

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.150
And so because this is a recent situation,

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.900
we don't have the samples necessary,

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.170
to kinda answer that question.

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:04.150
And so if someone's been
infected in February,

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:05.110
it's only been what,

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.134
three months?

00:03:06.134 --> 00:03:06.967
- [Pedro] Yeah.

00:03:06.967 --> 00:03:07.980
- And so we can't really predict

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:12.430
what their immune system is
gonna do in the next six months.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:14.420
But based on other infections

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:15.830
that are similar to SARS-CoV-2,

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:17.682
like SARS and MERS,

00:03:17.682 --> 00:03:22.370
immunity seems to be around a year or two.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:24.300
So scientists are kinda going off of that,

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.000
but once again we're still unsure.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:31.753
- [Pedro] That's great.

00:03:31.753 --> 00:03:34.830
Well, we'll move on to question three then

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:36.487
from Joan Gensert.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:41.210
He asks about subtypes of IgGs.

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:42.043
- Yes they are I think of...

00:03:42.043 --> 00:03:43.160
- [Pedro] You should talk
a little bit about this.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:46.050
- Yeah, so you're getting
really nitty gritty.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.340
So they are I think four subtypes of IgG.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:52.600
The authors do not indicate
what subtypes they are.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.633
I briefly read something,

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:59.440
no, I think that was something else.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.620
I'm not sure if I've read a paper that

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.440
specified the subtype of
IgG that they're looking at.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:05.911
It may be in the new paper

00:04:05.911 --> 00:04:08.810
that Rockefeller University put out,

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:11.170
because they actually
did a sequence analysis

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:13.000
of all the antibodies that are produced,

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.850
and I'm pretty sure they
might have a better idea

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:16.083
of what those subtypes of IgG are.

00:04:16.083 --> 00:04:18.480
And, so there are clonal differences

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.350
with the IgG antibodies that are produced,

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.380
and these clonal differences

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:25.440
do seem to have a connection with

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.660
how good they are at
neutralizing infection.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:30.010
But that's a good question.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:31.720
- All right cool.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.620
I think related maybe is Joan asks again,

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:36.130
was there a correlation between

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:39.450
the different seroconversion
times, I think

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.740
and the deaths versus survival

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:43.550
or released from the hospital.

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:44.780
And that's an interesting question

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:46.290
cause we didn't actually see

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:48.700
some correlation--

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:50.623
- Samples, yeah.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:53.310
So from the paper,

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:54.750
I don't think they,

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.603
I don't think any of the
patients succumbed to infection.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.140
So I don't know the answer to that,

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.720
so they did not follow the patients

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.920
after their release from the hospitals.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.930
So I can't, I can't actually
answer that question

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:14.373
based on what the authors presented, yeah.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:16.093
- This is an important question

00:05:16.093 --> 00:05:18.030
and I think you know folks are on it

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:20.054
and hopefully we'll get more data,

00:05:20.054 --> 00:05:22.563
-- give a bigger
picture of that later on.

00:05:24.845 --> 00:05:26.090
- [Pedro] Okay, that's great

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:29.750
and Aditi is back with another question.

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:31.470
Question five for the day.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.880
She asks if the IgG
antibodies are specific

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:37.720
to spike proteins or if
they're just a general

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:38.980
response of the virus.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:40.050
That's a really good question.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.427
- Yeah, that is a good question.

00:05:41.427 --> 00:05:43.760
So with the IgG antibodies,

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.860
so in this particular test,

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.490
the assay that they use,

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.140
the antigen that they use to detect

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.250
the IgG and IgM antibodies,

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.290
is an antigen that has both

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:56.510
parts of the spike protein,

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:58.683
and parts of the nuclear protein.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:03.340
And so it they're kinda
like lumping them in one.

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:04.780
So these are showing

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:06.547
that you can get IgG antibodies response

00:06:06.547 --> 00:06:09.440
to the spike protein
and the nuclear protein,

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.280
but I'm unsure of the
other parts of the virus.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:12.920
So it is specific,

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:15.550
and I'm not sure if
correlates with the different

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:17.580
types of IgG subtypes
that you're producing

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.930
or clonal antibodies
that you're producing.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:21.140
It's a good question.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:23.262
- Yeah, I think there's a test

00:06:23.262 --> 00:06:26.710
that some folks that have produced.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.400
I don't know who made this test,

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.560
but there's a test

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:32.360
that's an antibody test that's
specific to the N protein,

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:33.870
which is the nucleocapsid protein

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:35.420
that surrounds the RNA

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:37.010
and then the one that you talked about

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:38.390
which is specific to the S protein.

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:39.240
- So there are some

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:40.960
and I think the paper
addressed this a little

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:45.450
where they looked at cross reactivity of,

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:46.820
I think it's the N protein

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:50.180
of both SARS-CoV-1 and SARS-CoV-2,

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:52.003
and there seems to be
slight cross reactivity

00:06:52.003 --> 00:06:54.550
with a couple of the sample sets.

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:56.100
So they way they develop the assay

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:59.530
to bypass any like false positives,

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:02.110
they incorporated a peptide

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:06.040
that incorporates parts of
the N and parts of the S,

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:08.200
so they can weed out any
of the false positive

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:11.260
of antibodies that may
be there from a previous

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:15.160
SARS-type infection and
not the current SARS-CoV-2.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:18.260
The test I think right now
that Quest Diagnostic is using

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:20.173
and the one that I spoke about in,

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:24.707
in my first presentation they
were using the spike protein

00:07:24.707 --> 00:07:26.040
for those antibody tests

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:28.150
and I think the majority that,

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:31.590
or the few that FDA has
approved for emergency purposes,

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:32.877
do use that as protein.

00:07:32.877 --> 00:07:34.170
Or the spike protein.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:35.420
For the antibody testing.

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:37.123
- [Jeanne] Yeah it's so weird,

00:07:37.123 --> 00:07:39.360
one of the things I learnt
through this whole process

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:42.203
is how amazing antibodies are,

00:07:42.203 --> 00:07:43.377
like I'm like,

00:07:43.377 --> 00:07:45.470
"Oh great, they developed
an antibody test".

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:47.120
It never even occurred to me that

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:49.400
to ask, is this the right antibody test?

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:50.233
(laughs)

00:07:50.233 --> 00:07:51.066
- [Pedro] Yeah.

00:07:51.066 --> 00:07:52.500
- I feel like my mind is
blown through all of this.

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:53.970
- Yeah, no it's a lot.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:55.070
The immune response,

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:55.903
the immune system,

00:07:55.903 --> 00:07:57.430
the human body is fascinating.

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:59.390
Right, and then we're also combating that

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:01.020
with a fascinating virus.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:02.497
And so I think it's like a Catch-22

00:08:02.497 --> 00:08:04.330
trying to understand both.

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:06.900
- I know, I feel a little bit bad about

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:08.960
the level of excitement of science

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:10.100
I feel around something

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:12.170
that's so destructive to humans.

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:13.792
You know it's just like weird balance.

00:08:13.792 --> 00:08:14.670
The amount of--

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:15.616
- As a cancer, yeah.

00:08:15.616 --> 00:08:18.873
As a cancer researcher I can
definitely relate to that.

00:08:18.873 --> 00:08:21.870
- [Jeanne] Yeah, for sure.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:24.611
Great, so we have a question from Marva,

00:08:24.611 --> 00:08:25.761
- [Odaelys] Hey Marva.

00:08:25.761 --> 00:08:26.594
(laughs)

00:08:26.594 --> 00:08:27.630
- [Jeanne] I think that you're friends.

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:30.030
(laughs)

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.640
Can neutralizing antibodies
be made in the lab?

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:33.900
- [Odaelys] That's a really good question.

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:35.102
So that paper that I pointed out

00:08:35.102 --> 00:08:38.850
in the end that Rockefeller
put out about a week ago,

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:41.110
through the pre-print bioRxiv,

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:44.110
their goal is to somehow,

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:46.410
or is to make these antibodies in the lab.

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:47.330
So what they're looking at,

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:49.640
they're looking at the different patients.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:52.450
They're looking at their antibodies.

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:55.080
They're sequencing the
antibodies that they're have,

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:57.010
and they're trying to use that sequence

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:58.580
to produce the same antibodies

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:01.670
that seem to have really high
neutralizing capabilities.

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:04.770
And so if we can pretty
much replicate those

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:06.940
really strong neutralizing antibodies

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:09.280
that patients have produced
in their immune systems,

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:10.770
we can make more of that in the lab

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:12.060
to use as drug targets.

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:12.893
So that's exactly

00:09:12.893 --> 00:09:16.560
what scientists at Rockefeller
are working on right now.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:17.780
So yeah, we can.

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:19.730
- Yeah, I think it's the
Nussenzweig lab system--

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:20.563
- [Odaelys] Yeah Nussenzweig--

00:09:20.563 --> 00:09:23.260
- So look up Michel Nussenzweig's work.

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:25.300
They've been asking for people.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:26.590
There was a clinical study

00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:27.790
and it might still be ongoing,

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:30.120
that if anybody's recovered from COVID-19,

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:32.280
they wanna see what
your antibodies are like

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:34.520
so that they can base therapies

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:37.300
off of the super antibody producers.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:38.770
Right, so that's really--

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:40.106
it's all of that coming together.

00:09:40.106 --> 00:09:41.253
You know yeah.

00:09:41.253 --> 00:09:44.020
- It's kinda of relates to
some of the sort of earlier,

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:45.380
I don't know if people
are still working on that,

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:47.420
but some of the earlier studies of people

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:49.500
who were trying to get
serum out of patients,

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:52.790
who have recovered to use as
therapeutic itself, right?

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:53.910
- [Odaelys] Mm-hmm yeah,

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:56.430
so you're having patients who,

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:57.630
and I think the thought was like

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:00.330
if you had really bad
symptoms and you recovered,

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:01.910
that there's a high likelihood

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:03.660
that you have really good antibodies.

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:06.260
So they were asking patients
to donate their blood samples,

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:09.041
so personally I have
someone close to me who

00:10:09.041 --> 00:10:12.287
had COVID-19 and her
doctor asked her like,

00:10:12.287 --> 00:10:14.420
"Hey, can we have some of your blood

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:15.868
so we can use your serum?",

00:10:15.868 --> 00:10:18.770
hopefully to use it as a
treatment for other patients.

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:21.430
So, that's the whole idea behind it.

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:22.610
- Yeah, same for me.

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:24.700
I have a very close friend
who's actually a nurse.

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:26.010
And has been working in the ICUs,

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:27.070
who ended up with COVID

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:29.372
and she came from this from

00:10:29.372 --> 00:10:32.305
the angle of watching first hand people,

00:10:32.305 --> 00:10:36.560
you know suffer and many
not recover from the disease

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:38.180
and she was like take my blood

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:39.990
and do everything you can with it.

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:42.300
So, I think people are really looking to

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:44.276
to donate and under the circumstances.

00:10:44.276 --> 00:10:45.225
- [Pedro] Yeah, it's great

00:10:45.225 --> 00:10:47.550
that people are being that active as well.

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:48.400
- [Jeanne] So thank you to everybody

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:49.490
