00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:32.100
you know we can't have a,

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:35.110
you know a cold and flu medication

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:37.970
that's specific to our
bodies at this point in time,

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:41.410
though in cancer we wanna
have individualized, right?

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:43.560
And so there's sort of this push and pull

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:45.900
between the aggregate data

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:47.880
and understanding how the trends are,

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:50.610
so that we can predict what
happens in a population,

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:52.480
versus the individual response,

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:54.558
I think is a really fascinating way

00:12:54.558 --> 00:12:56.723
to approach the science here.

00:12:57.797 --> 00:12:58.630
- And even like if we're looking

00:12:58.630 --> 00:12:59.793
at the combined data set

00:12:59.793 --> 00:13:00.626
and the aggregate data.

00:13:00.626 --> 00:13:01.459
It's really nice because,

00:13:01.459 --> 00:13:03.830
like the bioRxiv
paper from Rockefeller,

00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:05.470
they're pinpointing individuals

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:08.940
who have this really high
neutralizing antibody response,

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:11.160
and using that particular individual

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:13.550
to utilize that for a population.

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:15.810
And so I think both are very important

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:18.600
for us to look at the
population on a whole,

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:20.570
but also if we can pinpoint someone

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:21.600
who has like,

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:23.080
you know slightly outlier

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:25.330
that we can hijack some
of their mechanisms

00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:27.690
to utilize for the rest of the population.

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:28.910
- Yeah am just thinking also

00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:31.440
historically when you look at outliers.

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:33.560
Like as an example,

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:35.340
there was a set of outliers

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:38.450
in like an island over
in the South Pacific,

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:40.763
that helped us to understand like

00:13:40.763 --> 00:13:43.290
HDL metabolism, right?

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:45.559
And sometimes these genetic outliers

00:13:45.559 --> 00:13:47.970
are open up doors for us to understand

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:50.490
some new phenomenon about the human body.

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:54.260
So it's really good that we
are taking all this data in,

00:13:54.260 --> 00:13:55.160
and that there's people

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:56.820
who can try to make sense of it

00:13:56.820 --> 00:13:58.010
in some way, shape, or form.

00:13:58.010 --> 00:13:58.843
And the style,

00:13:58.843 --> 00:14:02.240
and applying the different
styles of reasoning,

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:04.940
on to a question so that
there're multiple angles

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:07.020
being explored for any specific question.

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:09.080
I think is really important as well.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:10.350
- And the fact that it's,

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:11.670
you know as you mentioned earlier,

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:13.644
such a strong collaborate of effort

00:14:13.644 --> 00:14:16.320
involving different
hospitals and everything,

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.470
which is such an
important part of science.

